+# Decision: Change Delivery Approach
+
+## Context
+
+Committed edits on an event board must reach every connected staff browser quickly, so coordinators see stall closures and power problems while they still matter. A busy event has 10 to 25 editors, averages two status changes per minute, and bursts to 20 edits per second at opening time. Up to 800 browsers may be connected at once. Browsers that drop off the network for up to 20 minutes must recover the latest board on return.
+
+The choice sets the shape of three other parts of the system. The change store must keep replayable revisions and publish them reliably. Delivery must hold whatever connection or request surface the approach needs. Browser sync must reconnect and catch up in whatever way that surface supports. Getting this wrong means rebuilding those three parts, so the reasoning should survive for anyone who inherits them.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+### Gates
+
+- **Freshness target.** Committed changes appear in connected browsers within 15 seconds under normal connectivity. Subsecond delivery is useful but not required. *Inherited from the HLD goals.*
+- **Scale.** Supports 800 simultaneous browsers and opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second. *Inherited from the HLD goals.*
+- **Catch-up.** A browser disconnected for up to 20 minutes recovers the latest board, and the change store retains seven days of revisions to make that possible. *Inherited from the HLD goals.*
+- **Single authoritative revision.** Every committed edit has one event revision, regardless of delivery route. *Inherited from the HLD tenets.*
+- **Visible connectivity failure.** A dropped connection or failed fetch must be visible to the browser, not mistaken for an unchanged board. *Inherited from the HLD tenets.*
+
+All three options pass every gate. The gates are recorded so that a future reader knows the options were compared on equal footing, not that any of them was screened out.
+
+### Weighted Criteria
+
+- **Provider coupling** — *major.* How tightly the board's delivery depends on one vendor's service, API, and credentials, and how much work leaving that vendor would take. Set by the project's decision not to tie the board to a realtime vendor.
+- **Operational burden** — *major.* Ongoing work the project owns: connection capacity, reconnect behavior, replay after gaps, and on-call effort for the delivery path. The project has accepted a meaningful amount of this, so the criterion is weighed, not ignored.
+- **Recurring cost** — *moderate.* Fees paid every month for delivery, independent of usage the project already pays for.
+- **Update latency margin** — *moderate.* How far below the 15-second target typical delivery sits. A wider margin gives room for slow networks and bursts.
+- **Read load on the server** — *moderate.* Repeated requests that carry no new information, multiplied by browser count.
+- **Session authorization complexity** — *moderate.* Effort to authorize long-lived subscriptions, since command authorization re-checks event assignments and sessions expire after eight hours. Latent criterion, surfaced while probing for what a critic would weigh.
+- **Connectivity-failure visibility** — *minor.* Ease of making a dropped connection visible to the browser. Every option can meet the gate; this criterion ranks the effort involved.
+
+No criterion in this list derives from a tenet. The HLD tenets are gates here (single revision, visible failures, server-side authorization) and do not lean toward any option. See *Selection* for the consequence.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-Second HTTPS Polling
+
+Browsers request the board's changes every five seconds, passing the last revision they hold. The server answers with every committed revision after that cursor, or an empty set. Delivery is stateless request and response. The change store serves cursor queries over the revision log. Browsers run their own polling loop, with a retry policy and a visible error state when requests fail.
+
+- Gate, freshness: **passes**. Worst-case lag is about five seconds plus request time.
+- Gate, scale: **passes**. At 800 browsers, each polling every five seconds, that is about 160 reads per second, all of which return quickly when there is nothing new.
+- Gate, catch-up: **passes**. A browser sends its old cursor and receives everything since, subject to the seven-day retention.
+- Gate, single revision: **passes**. Revisions come from the same log regardless of route.
+- Gate, visible failure: **passes**. A failed request is an error the browser can show.
+- Provider coupling: **strong fit**. No external service is involved.
+- Operational burden: **strong fit**. No connection state to hold. Load is ordinary web traffic that the project already operates.
+- Recurring cost: **strong fit**. No additional service fees.
+- Update latency margin: **weak fit**. Typical lag is two to five seconds, well inside the target but with the least headroom of the three.
+- Read load on the server: **weak fit**. Most requests return nothing new, and the count grows linearly with browsers.
+- Session authorization complexity: **strong fit**. Each request is authorized like any other command or read.
+- Connectivity-failure visibility: **strong fit**. Failed requests are discrete and observable.
+
+*Summary:* the simplest operation, with the most repeated reads and the least latency headroom.
+
+### Self-Hosted WebSockets
+
+Browsers hold a long-lived socket to a delivery service the project runs. The change store publishes each committed revision reliably to delivery, which fans it out to subscribed sockets. Browsers record the last revision they applied. On a drop they reconnect with backoff and ask for everything after that revision before resuming live updates. Delivery owns socket session lifecycle and connection capacity.
+
+- Gate, freshness: **passes**. Revisions arrive as they are committed, typically well under one second.
+- Gate, scale: **passes**. 800 long-lived connections is a modest load for a socket server. Bursts of 20 edits per second fan out as 20 messages per second to each connected browser.
+- Gate, catch-up: **passes**. Requires the change store to serve revisions from a cursor, the same log polling would use.
+- Gate, single revision: **passes**. Each socket message carries the committed revision number.
+- Gate, visible failure: **passes**. A closed socket is an observable event, and browsers must show it.
+- Provider coupling: **strong fit**. The socket layer is the project's own code and infrastructure, so nothing is locked to a vendor's API.
+- Operational burden: **weak fit**. The project owns connection capacity, reconnect storms after an outage, heartbeats, and the reliable publication path from change store to delivery. These are ongoing responsibilities.
+- Recurring cost: **partial fit**. No vendor fee, but the project pays for the servers and their operation.
+- Update latency margin: **strong fit**. Typical delivery is well under the target, with headroom for bursts.
+- Read load on the server: **strong fit**. Only committed changes are sent, and nothing is re-read when nothing changed.
+- Session authorization complexity: **partial fit**. Subscriptions must be authorized at connect and must be revoked when an assignment is removed or a session expires. This is more work than authorizing discrete requests.
+- Connectivity-failure visibility: **partial fit**. Socket drops are visible, but the browser must distinguish a dropped socket from a quiet board, which takes heartbeats.
+
+*Summary:* fast updates and no vendor, paid for with connection and reconnect operations the project must own.
+
+### Hosted Realtime Service
+
+Browsers subscribe through a managed realtime provider's client library. The change store publishes committed revisions to the provider, and delivery wraps the provider's publish and subscribe calls. The change store must reconcile what it published with what the provider delivered, because the provider is a separate system that can fail independently. Browsers use the provider's subscription model, with its reconnect behavior, for live updates. Catch-up still runs against the project's own revision log.
+
+- Gate, freshness: **passes**. Typically the fastest option, with the provider handling fan-out.
+- Gate, scale: **passes**. Provider capacity is sized by the provider.
+- Gate, catch-up: **passes**. Catch-up reads the project's own log, so retention is unaffected by the provider.
+- Gate, single revision: **passes**, provided reconciliation ensures a committed revision is published once and recognized as committed. The project must do this work itself.
+- Gate, visible failure: **passes**. Provider outages must be surfaced by the browser, which needs to distinguish them from quiet periods.
+- Provider coupling: **weak fit**. Delivery depends on the provider's API, client library, and credentials. Moving away means rewriting delivery and browser sync.
+- Operational burden: **partial fit**. Connection capacity and fan-out are the provider's. The project still owns publication reconciliation, credential rotation, and the provider's failure modes.
+- Recurring cost: **weak fit**. Recurring fees scale with connections or messages, and the board's cost grows with use.
+- Update latency margin: **strong fit**. Typically the fastest of the three.
+- Read load on the server: **strong fit**. Same as self-hosted sockets: only changes are sent.
+- Session authorization complexity: **partial fit**. Subscription credentials are issued by the project and must be scoped to the event. Revocation depends on the provider honoring token expiry or a disconnect call.
+- Connectivity-failure visibility: **partial fit**. The provider's connection state is available, but its meaning is the provider's, not the project's.
+
+*Summary:* the quickest route to low latency and the least connection work, paid for with recurring fees and coupling to one provider.
+
+## Selection
+
+Self-hosted WebSockets is chosen.
+
+The decision turns on provider coupling and recurring cost against operational burden. Provider coupling is major, and the hosted option is weak on it, so hosted delivery is eliminated as the preferred route. Recurring cost is moderate and also favors self-hosting over hosted. Self-hosted sockets and hosted delivery are close on latency, and both beat polling, which has the least headroom.
+
+Operational burden is also major, and it cuts against the chosen option. Self-hosted sockets are weak on it. Polling is strong on it. Choosing sockets means accepting a larger operational surface than polling, specifically connection capacity, reconnect behavior after outages, heartbeats, and a reliable publication path, in exchange for update latency and lower repeated read load. The project accepts that trade on purpose, because the team judged that owning those responsibilities is cheaper than a provider's fees and coupling for this board's scale.
+
+Session authorization complexity also favors polling, and it is a real cost. Socket subscriptions must be authorized at connect and revoked when an assignment changes. The delivery node must specify this before it is built.
+
+No tenet is the lean here. The HLD tenets are gates for this decision, and the weighting comes from the goals and the project's stated preference against vendor coupling. A future reader who wants to reverse this choice should check whether the operational-burden tolerance or the provider-coupling priority has changed.
+
+### What this forecloses
+
+- Delivery through a provider's managed fan-out, unless the provider is later wrapped behind the same socket contract.
+- Polling as the primary route. It may still exist as a fallback for clients that cannot hold a socket, but that would be a second delivery path and needs its own decision.
+
+### What this obligates downstream
+
+- **Change store:** replayable revisions served from a cursor, and a reliable publication path from each commit to delivery, such that no committed revision is lost if delivery is down.
+- **Delivery:** socket session lifecycle, heartbeats, connection capacity planning for 800 browsers, reconnect-storm handling after outages, and subscription authorization scoped to event assignments and revoked on assignment change or session expiry.
+- **Browser sync:** reconnect with backoff, last-applied-revision tracking, catch-up before resuming live updates, and a visible state when the socket is down.
+- **Operations:** load tests at 800 connections and 20 messages per second per burst, and alerting on connection counts and reconnect rates.
